Section 1 : Topic : Getting Started and Cleaning the Data (Track B Week 2 Prep)
\f1\b0  
\f0\b Summary:
\f1\b0  This section sets up the project by installing important software libraries like pypdf (to read PDFs), scikit-learn (for machine learning), and pandas and matplotlib (for organizing and drawing charts). It then takes a massive 1,338-page GitLab HR Handbook and cleans it up by removing useless headers, footers, and page numbers. The code chops this giant book into 502 neat, bite-sized documents (like doc_001 to doc_502). This prepares the clean text documents required for the Week 2 RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) corpus deliverable.\

\f0\b Section 2 : Topic : Grouping Documents by Topic (Track A)
\f1\b0  
\f0\b Summary:
\f1\b0  The notebook uses a machine learning strategy called TF-IDF and NMF to read all 502 documents and automatically sort them into 5 main HR categories (like "Time Off & Benefits" or "Hiring"). It calculates a "confidence score" to show how strongly a document belongs to a topic, draws a bar chart to visualize the results, and saves all the cleaned files into a downloaded FINAL.zip folder so they are ready for the search engine.\

\f0\b Section 3 : Topic : Setting Up the Test and the AI (Track B Week 1 Deliverable)
\f1\b0  
\f0\b Summary:
\f1\b0  The code prepares to use the Qwen/Qwen3-8B AI model (an API-hosted model under 8 billion parameters, as required by the project). It loads a test file named gitlab_eval_questions.json which contains 80 realistic HR questions paired with their correct "gold answers". This directly fulfills the Week 1 requirement to create an evaluation set of 60-100 realistic queries before writing the main agent code. It also sets up a math formula (SS.png) that will be used to grade the AI's accuracy.\

\f0\b Section 4 : Topic : Testing Different Prompt Strategies (Track B Week 1 Deliverable)
\f1\b0  
\f0\b Summary:
\f1\b0  This section tests how different ways of talking to the AI change its answers, directly addressing the Week 1 prompt engineering baseline requirement. It compares basic, simple instructions ("Naive Prompts") against highly engineered, smart instructions ("Optimized RAG Prompts") across strategies like Few-Shot, Chain-of-Thought, and Decomposition. The optimized strategy uses a few clever tricks: it wraps the text in XML tags so the AI doesn't get confused, uses a "Lost-in-the-Middle" trick (putting the most important info at the very top and very bottom so the AI remembers it), and forces the AI to say "Insufficient information" instead of making up lies.\

\f0\b Section 5 : Topic : Grading the AI and Fixing Mistakes (Track B Week 1 & 2 Deliverable)
\f1\b0  
\f0\b Summary:
\f1\b0  The final section acts as an automated teacher that grades the AI's answers on accuracy, faithfulness (how well it stuck to the facts), and whether it provided valid citations. Fulfilling the Week 1 requirement to document how small models break, it diagnoses specific failures\'97such as the AI blindly copying numbers from examples (Template Contamination) or doing bad math (Logical Hallucination). Finally, it generates a report proving that the optimized RAG strategy fixed these mistakes, forcing the AI to extract exact quotes into a <Scratchpad> before answering, which boosted accuracy to over 90% and satisfied the Week 2 requirement for citation-grounded answers.\
